# Deployment floors — decide these, and nothing will decide them for you

No package created by the scaffold carries a `platforms:` line unless this repo already answered the
question. That is deliberate: a floor written by a tool is a number nobody chose, and it compiles
happily until the first shared-code change fails against it.

## Read the real answer, never a doc

```bash
./Scripts/detect-toolchain.sh              # what the machine has, what the project says
./Scripts/detect-toolchain.sh --markdown   # the same, as a table
```

A **project** source means the answer exists — it lives in a `Package.swift` `platforms:` line, and
that manifest is what the compiler obeys. A **machine** source is an SDK version, which is not a
deployment floor: it says what you can build with, never what you must support.

## Setting them

Add one line per package manifest, the same values in every one:

```swift
platforms: [.iOS(.v17), .macOS("15.0")],   // your numbers, chosen — not these
```

Then `./Scripts/check.sh` fails whenever they drift apart, and
`.claude/notes/PROJECT.md` records what they resolved to.

## The asymmetry that decides the numbers

The Mac floor sits many OS generations above the iPhone floor, and **shared code compiles against the
lower one**. So:

- An API available on your Mac floor but not your iOS floor breaks the iOS build, even where the Mac
  target would accept it.
- Reach a newer API with `#if os(macOS)` (platform-exclusive) or `if #available` (with a working
  fallback) — never by raising a floor.
- Every gate lives **inside a DesignSystem component or an infrastructure wrapper**. A feature must
  not know which OS it is on.

## Choosing

| Question | What it costs |
|---|---|
| How far back do your users actually go? | Analytics answers this; a guess buys you compatibility work nobody needed |
| iOS only for now? | Then write no macOS floor at all. Adding Mac later means auditing every view for gaps — cheaper than carrying a floor you never tested against |
| Chasing the newest APIs? | Each one you reach for without a fallback is a user you dropped. Gate it instead |

Record the answer with `/decide` once, and delete this file — it is a decision aid, not documentation.
